if(A.e5(k,A.a(["diabetes","diabetic","sugar","hba1c","glucose","insulin","sugar diet","blood sugar"],j))){if(b)return"### \u0c2e\u0c27\u0c41\u0c2e\u0c47\u0c39 \u0c15\u0c4d\u0c32\u0c3f\u0c28\u0c3f\u0c15\u0c32\u0c4d \u0c21\u0c48\u0c1f\u0c4d \u0c2e\u0c3e\u0c30\u0c4d\u0c17\u0c26\u0c30\u0c4d\u0c36\u0c15\u0c3e\u0c32\u0c41 (Type-2 / Pre-diabetes)\n\n| \u0c2a\u0c4b\u0c37\u0c15\u0c02 | \u0c38\u0c3f\u0c2b\u0c3e\u0c30\u0c4d\u0c38\u0c41 \u0c2a\u0c30\u0c3f\u0c2e\u0c3e\u0c23\u0c02 | \u0c2a\u0c4d\u0c30\u0c27\u0c3e\u0c28 \u0c35\u0c28\u0c30\u0c41\u0c32\u0c41 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| \u0c15\u0c3e\u0c30\u0c4d\u0c2c\u0c4b\u0c39\u0c48\u0c21\u0c4d\u0c30\u0c47\u0c1f\u0c4d\u0c32\u0c41 | 45-50% (\u0c24\u0c15\u0c4d\u0c15\u0c41\u0c35 GI) | \u0c1c\u0c4a\u0c28\u0c4d\u0c28\u0c32\u0c41, \u0c30\u0c3e\u0c17\u0c41\u0c32\u0c41, \u0c26\u0c02\u0c2a\u0c41\u0c21\u0c41 \u0c2c\u0c3f\u0c2f\u0c4d\u0c2f\u0c02, \u0c2c\u0c4d\u0c30\u0c4c\u0c28\u0c4d \u0c30\u0c48\u0c38\u0c4d |\n| \u0c2a\u0c40\u0c1a\u0c41 \u0c2a\u0c26\u0c3e\u0c30\u0c4d\u0c27\u0c02 (Fiber) | 35g - 45g / \u0c30\u0c4b\u0c1c\u0c41\u0c15\u0c41 | \u0c2e\u0c46\u0c02\u0c24\u0c41\u0c32\u0c41, \u0c15\u0c3e\u0c15\u0c30\u0c15\u0c3e\u0c2f, \u0c06\u0c15\u0c41\u0c15\u0c42\u0c30\u0c32\u0c41, \u0c2c\u0c46\u0c02\u0c21\u0c15\u0c3e\u0c2f |\n| \u0c2a\u0c4d\u0c30\u0c4b\u0c1f\u0c40\u0c28\u0c4d | 20-25% | \u0c2a\u0c46\u0c38\u0c32\u0c41, \u0c2e\u0c3f\u0c28\u0c2a\u0c4d\u0c2a\u0c2a\u0c4d\u0c2a\u0c41, \u0c2a\u0c28\u0c4d\u0c28\u0c40\u0c30\u0c4d, \u0c17\u0c41\u0c21\u0c4d\u0c21\u0c41 \u0c24\u0c46\u0c32\u0c4d\u0c32\u0c38\u0c4a\u0c28 |\n| \u0c06\u0c30\u0c4b\u0c17\u0c4d\u0c2f\u0c15\u0c30\u0c2e\u0c48\u0c28 \u0c15\u0c4a\u0c35\u0c4d\u0c35\u0c41\u0c32\u0c41 | 20-25% | \u0c06\u0c32\u0c3f\u0c35\u0c4d \u0c06\u0c2f\u0c3f\u0c32\u0c4d, \u0c35\u0c47\u0c30\u0c41\u0c36\u0c46\u0c28\u0c17\u0c32\u0c41, \u0c05\u0c35\u0c3f\u0c38\u0c46 \u0c17\u0c3f\u0c02\u0c1c\u0c32\u0c41 |\n\n**\u0c2e\u0c41\u0c16\u0c4d\u0c2f \u0c15\u0c4d\u0c32\u0c3f\u0c28\u0c3f\u0c15\u0c32\u0c4d \u0c38\u0c42\u0c1a\u0c28\u0c32\u0c41**:\n- **\u0c17\u0c4d\u0c32\u0c48\u0c38\u0c46\u0c2e\u0c3f\u0c15\u0c4d \u0c28\u0c3f\u0c2f\u0c02\u0c24\u0c4d\u0c30\u0c23**: \u0c09\u0c26\u0c2f\u0c02 \u0c2a\u0c30\u0c17\u0c21\u0c41\u0c2a\u0c41\u0c28 1 \u0c38\u0c4d\u0c2a\u0c42\u0c28\u0c4d \u0c28\u0c3e\u0c28\u0c2c\u0c46\u0c1f\u0c4d\u0c1f\u0c3f\u0c28 \u0c2e\u0c46\u0c02\u0c24\u0c3f \u0c28\u0c40\u0c30\u0c41 \u0c24\u0c4d\u0c30\u0c3e\u0c17\u0c02\u0c21\u0c3f.\n- **\u0c2d\u0c4b\u0c1c\u0c28 \u0c38\u0c2e\u0c2f\u0c3e\u0c32\u0c41**: \u0c2d\u0c4b\u0c1c\u0c28\u0c02 \u0c12\u0c15\u0c47\u0c38\u0c3e\u0c30\u0c3f \u0c0e\u0c15\u0c4d\u0c15\u0c41\u0c35\u0c17\u0c3e \u0c24\u0c40\u0c38\u0c41\u0c15\u0c4b\u0c15\u0c41\u0c02\u0c21\u0c3e 3 \u0c2a\u0c4d\u0c30\u0c27\u0c3e\u0c28 \u0c2d\u0c4b\u0c1c\u0c28\u0c3e\u0c32\u0c41, 2 \u0c1a\u0c3f\u0c28\u0c4d\u0c28 \u0c38\u0c4d\u0c28\u0c3e\u0c15\u0c4d\u0c38\u0c4d\u200c\u0c17\u0c3e \u0c35\u0c3f\u0c2d\u0c1c\u0c3f\u0c02\u0c1a\u0c02\u0c21\u0c3f.\n- **\u0c28\u0c3f\u0c37\u0c47\u0c27\u0c3e\u0c32\u0c41**: \u0c2e\u0c48\u0c26\u0c3e, \u0c24\u0c46\u0c32\u0c4d\u0c32 \u0c2a\u0c02\u0c1a\u0c26\u0c3e\u0c30, \u0c2a\u0c4d\u0c2f\u0c3e\u0c15\u0c4d \u0c1a\u0c47\u0c38\u0c3f\u0c28 \u0c2a\u0c02\u0c21\u0c4d\u0c32 \u0c30\u0c38\u0c3e\u0c32\u0c41 \u0c2a\u0c42\u0c30\u0c4d\u0c24\u0c3f\u0c17\u0c3e \u0c28\u0c3f\u0c35\u0c3e\u0c30\u0c3f\u0c02\u0c1a\u0c02\u0c21\u0c3f.\n\n*\u0c35\u0c48\u0c26\u0c4d\u0c2f \u0c17\u0c2e\u0c28\u0c3f\u0c15: \u0c07\u0c28\u0c4d\u0c38\u0c41\u0c32\u0c3f\u0c28\u0c4d \u0c32\u0c47\u0c26\u0c3e \u0c17\u0c4d\u0c32\u0c42\u0c15\u0c4b\u0c1c\u0c4d \u0c2e\u0c02\u0c26\u0c41\u0c32\u0c41 \u0c35\u0c3e\u0c21\u0c47\u0c35\u0c3e\u0c30\u0c41 \u0c39\u0c48\u0c2a\u0c4b\u0c17\u0c4d\u0c32\u0c48\u0c38\u0c40\u0c2e\u0c3f\u0c2f\u0c3e \u0c30\u0c3e\u0c15\u0c41\u0c02\u0c21\u0c3e \u0c30\u0c15\u0c4d\u0c24\u0c02\u0c32\u0c4b \u0c1a\u0c15\u0c4d\u0c15\u0c46\u0c30 \u0c38\u0c4d\u0c25\u0c3e\u0c2f\u0c3f\u0c32\u0c28\u0c41 \u0c15\u0c4d\u0c30\u0c2e\u0c02 \u0c24\u0c2a\u0c4d\u0c2a\u0c15\u0c41\u0c02\u0c21\u0c3e \u0c2a\u0c30\u0c40\u0c15\u0c4d\u0c37\u0c3f\u0c02\u0c1a\u0c41\u0c15\u0c4b\u0c02\u0c21\u0c3f.*"
return"### Clinical Nutrition Protocol for Diabetes & Glycemic Control (Type 2 & Pre-diabetes)\n\n| Macronutrient | Daily Target | Evidence-Based Food Sources |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Carbohydrates | 45-50% (Low Glycemic Index) | Millets (Jowar, Ragi, Foxtail), Steel-cut Oats, Whole Lentils |\n| Dietary Fiber | 35g - 45g / day | Fenugreek (Methi), Bitter Gourd (Karela), Psyllium, Leafy Greens |\n| Protein | 20-25% (Preserves Beta-Cells) | Moong dal, Boiled Eggs / Whites, Tofu, Low-Fat Curd, Soya Chunks |\n| Healthy Fats | 20-25% (MUFA/PUFA) | Cold-pressed Mustard/Groundnut oil, Flaxseeds, Walnuts |\n\n**Clinical Action Points**:\n- **Fiber Preload**: Consume 1 bowl of raw salad (cucumber, tomato) or 1 glass warm fenugreek-infused water 15 minutes before lunch.\n- **Portion Architecture**: Follow the Diabetes Plate Method: 50% non-starchy vegetables, 25% lean protein, 25% complex carbs.\n- **Strictly Prohibited**: Refined flour (maida), fruit juices (lacks fiber, causes rapid glucose spikes), and sweetened tea/coffee.\n\n*Clinical Note: If you take Metformin, Sulfonylureas, or Insulin, never skip meals to prevent hypoglycemia. Coordinate with your endocrinologist.*"}if(A.e5(k,A.a(["bp","blood pressure","hypertension","dash","high bp","salt","sodium"],j))){if(b)return"### \u0c05\u0c27\u0c3f\u0c15 \u0c30\u0c15\u0c4d\u0c24\u0c2a\u0c4b\u0c1f\u0c41 (BP) \u0c15\u0c4a\u0c30\u0c15\u0c41 DASH \u0c15\u0c4d\u0c32\u0c3f\u0c28\u0c3f\u0c15\u0c32\u0c4d \u0c21\u0c48\u0c1f\u0c4d\n\n| \u0c2a\u0c4b\u0c37\u0c15\u0c02 | \u0c38\u0c3f\u0c2b\u0c3e\u0c30\u0c4d\u0c38\u0c41 \u0c2a\u0c30\u0c3f\u0c2e\u0c3f\u0c24\u0c3f | \u0c06\u0c39\u0c3e\u0c30 \u0c35\u0c28\u0c30\u0c41\u0c32\u0c41 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| \u0c38\u0c4b\u0c21\u0c3f\u0c2f\u0c02 (\u0c09\u0c2a\u0c4d\u0c2a\u0c41) | < 1,500 - 2,000 mg/\u0c30\u0c4b\u0c1c\u0c41\u0c15\u0c41 (< 1 \u0c1a\u0c46\u0c02\u0c1a\u0c3e \u0c09\u0c2a\u0c4d\u0c2a\u0c41) | \u0c28\u0c3f\u0c2e\u0c4d\u0c2e\u0c30\u0c38\u0c02, \u0c1c\u0c40\u0c32\u0c15\u0c30\u0c4d\u0c30 \u0c2a\u0c4a\u0c21\u0c3f\u0c24\u0c4b \u0c30\u0c41\u0c1a\u0c3f\u0c28\u0c3f \u0c2a\u0c46\u0c02\u0c1a\u0c02\u0c21\u0c3f |\n| \u0c2a\u0c4a\u0c1f\u0c3e\u0c37\u0c3f\u0c2f\u0c02 | 3,500 - 4,700 mg/\u0c30\u0c4b\u0c1c\u0c41\u0c15\u0c41 | \u0c32\u0c47\u0c24 \u0c15\u0c4a\u0c2c\u0c4d\u0c2c\u0c30\u0c3f \u0c28\u0c40\u0c30\u0c41, \u0c05\u0c30\u0c1f\u0c3f\u0c2a\u0c02\u0c21\u0c41, \u0c2a\u0c3e\u0c32\u0c15\u0c42\u0c30, \u0c2c\u0c24\u0c4d\u0c24\u0c3e\u0c2f\u0c3f |\n| \u0c2e\u0c46\u0c17\u0c4d\u0c28\u0c40\u0c37\u0c3f\u0c2f\u0c02 | 400 - 420 mg/\u0c30\u0c4b\u0c1c\u0c41\u0c15\u0c41 | \u0c17\u0c41\u0c2e\u0c4d\u0c2e\u0c21\u0c3f \u0c17\u0c3f\u0c02\u0c1c\u0c32\u0c41, \u0c2c\u0c3e\u0c26\u0c02, \u0c2e\u0c41\u0c26\u0c41\u0c30\u0c41 \u0c06\u0c15\u0c41\u0c15\u0c42\u0c30\u0c32\u0c41 |\n| \u0c15\u0c3e\u0c32\u0c4d\u0c37\u0c3f\u0c2f\u0c02 | 1,000 - 1,200 mg/\u0c30\u0c4b\u0c1c\u0c41\u0c15\u0c41 | \u0c24\u0c3e\u0c1c\u0c3e \u0c2e\u0c1c\u0c4d\u0c1c\u0c3f\u0c17, \u0c24\u0c15\u0c4d\u0c15\u0c41\u0c35 \u0c15\u0c4a\u0c35\u0c4d\u0c35\u0c41 \u0c2a\u0c46\u0c30\u0c41\u0c17\u0c41 |\n\n**\u0c2e\u0c41\u0c16\u0c4d\u0c2f \u0c28\u0c3f\u0c35\u0c3e\u0c30\u0c23\u0c32\u0c41**:\n- \u0c0a\u0c30\u0c17\u0c3e\u0c2f\u0c32\u0c41 (\u0c2a\u0c1a\u0c4d\u0c1a\u0c33\u0c4d\u0c33\u0c41), \u0c05\u0c2a\u0c4d\u0c2a\u0c21\u0c3e\u0c32\u0c41, \u0c2c\u0c47\u0c15\u0c3f\u0c02\u0c17\u0c4d \u0c38\u0c4b\u0c21\u0c3e, \u0c28\u0c3f\u0c32\u0c4d\u0c35 \u0c09\u0c02\u0c1a\u0c3f\u0c28 \u0c24\u0c3f\u0c28\u0c41\u0c2c\u0c02\u0c21\u0c3e\u0c30\u0c3e\u0c32\u0c41 \u0c2a\u0c42\u0c30\u0c4d\u0c24\u0c3f\u0c17\u0c3e \u0c2e\u0c3e\u0c28\u0c02\u0c21\u0c3f.\n- \u0c30\u0c4b\u0c1c\u0c42 1 \u0c17\u0c4d\u0c32\u0c3e\u0c38\u0c41 \u0c24\u0c3e\u0c1c\u0c3e \u0c2e\u0c1c\u0c4d\u0c1c\u0c3f\u0c17\u0c32\u0c4b \u0c2a\u0c41\u0c26\u0c40\u0c28\u0c3e, \u0c35\u0c47\u0c2f\u0c3f\u0c02\u0c1a\u0c3f\u0c28 \u0c1c\u0c40\u0c32\u0c15\u0c30\u0c4d\u0c30 \u0c2a\u0c4a\u0c21\u0c3f \u0c15\u0c32\u0c3f\u0c2a\u0c3f \u0c24\u0c4d\u0c30\u0c3e\u0c17\u0c21\u0c02 \u0c0e\u0c02\u0c24\u0c4b \u0c2e\u0c47\u0c32\u0c41.\n\n*\u0c35\u0c48\u0c26\u0c4d\u0c2f \u0c17\u0c2e\u0c28\u0c3f\u0c15: \u0c2c\u0c40\u0c2a\u0c40 \u0c2e\u0c02\u0c26\u0c41\u0c32\u0c41 (ACE inhibitors/ARBs) \u0c35\u0c3e\u0c21\u0c47\u0c35\u0c3e\u0c30\u0c41 \u0c2a\u0c4a\u0c1f\u0c3e\u0c37\u0c3f\u0c2f\u0c02 \u0c38\u0c2a\u0c4d\u0c32\u0c3f\u0c2e\u0c46\u0c02\u0c1f\u0c4d\u0c32\u0c28\u0c41 \u0c21\u0c3e\u0c15\u0c4d\u0c1f\u0c30\u0c4d \u0c38\u0c32\u0c39\u0c3e \u0c32\u0c47\u0c15\u0c41\u0c02\u0c21\u0c3e \u0c24\u0c40\u0c38\u0c41\u0c15\u0c4b\u0c15\u0c42\u0c21\u0c26\u0c41.*"
return"### Clinical DASH Protocol for Hypertension & Blood Pressure Regulation\n\n| Nutrient Metric | Clinical Limit | Recommended Food Sources |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Sodium | < 1,500 - 2,000 mg/day (< 1 tsp salt) | Use herbs, lemon juice, roasted jeera for flavor |\n| Potassium | 3,500 - 4,700 mg/day | Tender coconut water, Bananas, Spinach, Muskmelon |\n| Magnesium | 400 - 420 mg/day | Pumpkin seeds, Almonds, Dark leafy greens |\n| Calcium | 1,000 - 1,200 mg/day | Fresh homemade buttermilk (Majjiga), Low-fat curd |\n\n**Clinical Action Points**:\n- **Sodium Control**: Eliminate pickles (pachadi), papads, baking soda, packaged namkeens, and instant soup mixes immediately.\n- **Daily Electrolyte Balance**: 1 glass of fresh buttermilk with crushed mint and cumin seeds provides natural potassium and calcium without sodium overload.\n- **Vascular Health**: Consume 1-2 raw garlic cloves crushed in the morning to support nitric oxide synthesis and endothelial relaxation.\n\n*Clinical Note: If taking ACE inhibitors or ARBs, monitor serum potassium before supplementing potassium salts.*"}if(A.e5(k,A.a(["kidney","ckd","renal","creatinine","glomerular","proteinuria","kidney stone"],j)))return"### Clinical Renal Nutrition Protocol (Kidney Health & CKD Management)\n\n| Clinical Parameter | Stage 3-5 Non-Dialysis Target | Mechanism & Clinical Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Dietary Protein | 0.6g - 0.8g / kg body weight | Prevents glomerular hyperfiltration & nitrogen waste accumulation |\n| Sodium | < 1,800 - 2,000 mg/day | Controls fluid retention, edema, and secondary hypertension |\n| Potassium | Monitored (< 2,000 mg/day if elevated) | Prevents cardiac arrhythmias in reduced renal excretion |\n| Phosphorus | < 800 - 1,000 mg/day | Protects against secondary hyperparathyroidism & vascular calcification |\n\n**Renal Dietary Rules**:\n- **High Biological Value (HBV)**: Consume at least 50% of permitted protein from HBV sources (egg whites, paneer in measured quantities).\n- **Leaching Technique**: Chop and boil high-potassium tubers (potatoes) in excess water, discarding the water before cooking.\n- **Strict Avoidance**: Dark colas, beer, processed cheese, packaged instant gravies, and excessive raw dry fruits.\n\n*Clinical Alert: Patients on hemodialysis require elevated protein (~1.2g/kg). Exact restrictions must be supervised by your treating Nephrologist.*"
if(A.e5(k,A.a(["fatty liver","nafld","sgot","sgpt","liver enzymes","steatosis","hepatic"],j)))return"### Clinical Nutrition Protocol for Fatty Liver (NAFLD) & Hepatic Healing\n\n| Nutritional Target | Recommendation | Mechanism & Benefit |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Zero Added Fructose | Cut soda, bakery items, juices | Fructose directly converts to liver triglycerides via de novo lipogenesis |\n| Alcohol Intake | 0.0% (Strict Abstinence) | Prevents second-hit oxidative stress and fibrotic progression |\n| Moderate Caloric Deficit | 500 kcal deficit (~0.5 kg/week) | 7-10% body weight loss reduces hepatic fat by over 50% |\n| Hepatoprotective Agents | Black coffee (2 cups/day), Green tea | Chlorogenic acid and EGCG reduce hepatic collagen deposition & inflammation |\n\n**Actionable Hepatic Steps**:\n- **Omega-3 Fatty Acids**: Consume 1 tbsp flaxseed powder or walnuts daily to dampen hepatic inflammation.\n- **Complex Fiber**: Soluble fiber from oats, fenugreek, and green leafy vegetables binds intestinal lipids and reduces fatty liver severity.\n- **Physical Activity**: Minimum 150 minutes/week of brisk walking or resistance training to restore hepatic insulin sensitivity.\n\n*Clinical Note: Reversing grade 1 and grade 2 fatty liver is 100% achievable through structured nutritional lifestyle therapy.*"
if(A.e5(k,A.a(["high protein","protein sources","protein food","protein diet","how much protein","protein daily"],j))){if(b)return"### \u0c2a\u0c4d\u0c30\u0c3e\u0c2e\u0c3e\u0c23\u0c3f\u0c15 \u0c15\u0c4d\u0c32\u0c3f\u0c28\u0c3f\u0c15\u0c32\u0c4d \u0c2a\u0c4d\u0c30\u0c4b\u0c1f\u0c40\u0c28\u0c4d \u0c06\u0c39\u0c3e\u0c30\u0c3e\u0c32 \u0c1c\u0c3e\u0c2c\u0c3f\u0c24\u0c3e (ICMR-NIN & USDA \u0c27\u0c43\u0c35\u0c40\u0c15\u0c30\u0c3f\u0c02\u0c1a\u0c3f\u0c28\u0c35\u0c3f)\n\n| \u0c06\u0c39\u0c3e\u0c30\u0c02 | \u0c2a\u0c4d\u0c30\u0c3e\u0c2e\u0c3e\u0c23\u0c3f\u0c15 \u0c2a\u0c30\u0c3f\u0c2e\u0c3e\u0c23\u0c02 | \u0c2a\u0c4d\u0c30\u0c4b\u0c1f\u0c40\u0c28\u0c4d | \u0c15\u0c47\u0c32\u0c30\u0c40\u0c32\u0c41 | \u0c15\u0c3e\u0c30\u0c4d\u0c2c\u0c4b\u0c39\u0c48\u0c21\u0c4d\u0c30\u0c47\u0c1f\u0c4d\u0c32\u0c41 | \u0c15\u0c4a\u0c35\u0c4d\u0c35\u0c41 |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| \u0c15\u0c4b\u0c21\u0c3f\u0c17\u0c41\u0c21\u0c4d\u0c32\u0c41 (\u0c09\u0c21\u0c15\u0c2c\u0c46\u0c1f\u0c4d\u0c1f\u0c3f\u0c28\u0c35\u0c3f) | 2 \u0c2a\u0c46\u0c26\u0c4d\u0c26 \u0c17\u0c41\u0c21\u0c4d\u0c32\u0c41 (100g) | **12.6g** | 144 kcal | 0.8g | 10.0g |\n| \u0c17\u0c41\u0c21\u0c4d\u0c21\u0c41 \u0c24\u0c46\u0c32\u0c4d\u0c32\u0c38\u0c4a\u0c28 | 3 \u0c24\u0c46\u0c32\u0c4d\u0c32\u0c38\u0c4a\u0c28\u0c32\u0c41 (100g) | **11.0g** | 52 kcal | 0.7g | 0.2g |\n| \u0c1a\u0c3f\u0c15\u0c46\u0c28\u0c4d \u0c2c\u0c4d\u0c30\u0c46\u0c38\u0c4d\u0c1f\u0c4d (\u0c09\u0c21\u0c3f\u0c15\u0c3f\u0c02\u0c1a\u0c3f\u0c28\u0c26\u0c3f) | 100g \u0c2c\u0c4b\u0c28\u0c4d\u200c\u0c32\u0c46\u0c38\u0c4d | **31.0g** | 165 kcal | 0.0g | 3.6g |\n| \u0c24\u0c3e\u0c1c\u0c3e \u0c06\u0c35\u0c41 \u0c2a\u0c28\u0c4d\u0c28\u0c40\u0c30\u0c4d | 100g | **18.3g** | 265 kcal | 3.5g | 20.8g |\n| \u0c38\u0c4b\u0c2f\u0c3e \u0c1a\u0c02\u0c15\u0c4d\u0c38\u0c4d (\u0c2a\u0c1a\u0c4d\u0c1a\u0c3f\u0c35\u0c3f) | 50g Raw (150g \u0c05\u0c35\u0c41\u0c24\u0c41\u0c02\u0c26\u0c3f) | **26.0g** | 172 kcal | 16.5g | 0.3g |\n| \u0c2a\u0c2a\u0c4d\u0c2a\u0c41 (\u0c15\u0c02\u0c26\u0c3f/\u0c2a\u0c46\u0c38\u0c30) | 1 \u0c15\u0c2a\u0c4d\u0c2a\u0c41 \u0c09\u0c21\u0c3f\u0c15\u0c3f\u0c02\u0c1a\u0c3f\u0c28\u0c26\u0c3f (150g) | **8.5g** | 148 kcal | 22.0g | 2.5g |\n| \u0c09\u0c21\u0c15\u0c2c\u0c46\u0c1f\u0c4d\u0c1f\u0c3f\u0c28 \u0c28\u0c32\u0c4d\u0c32 \u0c36\u0c28\u0c17\u0c32\u0c41 | 1 \u0c15\u0c2a\u0c4d\u0c2a\u0c41 (150g) | **11.0g** | 210 kcal | 35.0g | 3.0g |\n| \u0c2e\u0c4a\u0c32\u0c15\u0c32 \u0c38\u0c32\u0c3e\u0c21\u0c4d | 1 \u0c15\u0c2a\u0c4d\u0c2a\u0c41 (100g) | **7.5g** | 110 kcal | 18.0g | 0.8g |\n| \u0c24\u0c3e\u0c1c\u0c3e \u0c2a\u0c46\u0c30\u0c41\u0c17\u0c41 | 1 \u0c15\u0c2a\u0c4d\u0c2a\u0c41 (200g) | **6.5g** | 122 kcal | 9.0g | 6.5g |\n\n*\u0c15\u0c4d\u0c32\u0c3f\u0c28\u0c3f\u0c15\u0c32\u0c4d \u0c32\u0c46\u0c15\u0c4d\u0c15: 1g \u0c2a\u0c4d\u0c30\u0c4b\u0c1f\u0c40\u0c28\u0c4d = 4 kcal, 1g \u0c2a\u0c3f\u0c02\u0c21\u0c3f\u0c2a\u0c26\u0c3e\u0c30\u0c4d\u0c27\u0c02 = 4 kcal, 1g \u0c15\u0c4a\u0c35\u0c4d\u0c35\u0c41 = 9 kcal.*"
return"### Verified Clinical Protein Database (ICMR-NIN & USDA Validated)\n\n| Food Item | Standard Portion | Protein | Calories | Carbs | Fat |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Whole Eggs (Boiled) | 2 Large Eggs (100g) | **12.6g** | 144 kcal | 0.8g | 10.0g |\n| Egg Whites | 3 Whites (100g) | **11.0g** | 52 kcal | 0.7g | 0.2g |\n| Chicken Breast (Cooked) | 100g Boneless | **31.0g** | 165 kcal | 0.0g | 3.6g |\n| Paneer (Fresh Cow Milk) | 100g | **18.3g** | 265 kcal | 3.5g | 20.8g |\n| Soya Chunks (Dry) | 50g Raw (expands to 150g) | **26.0g** | 172 kcal | 16.5g | 0.3g |\n| Toor / Moong Dal (Cooked) | 1 Cup (150g) | **8.5g** | 148 kcal | 22.0g | 2.5g |\n| Boiled Kala Chana | 1 Cup (150g) | **11.0g** | 210 kcal | 35.0g | 3.0g |\n| Sprouted Moong Salad | 1 Cup (100g) | **7.5g** | 110 kcal | 18.0g | 0.8g |\n| Fresh Curd / Dahi | 1 Cup (200g) | **6.5g** | 122 kcal | 9.0g | 6.5g |\n\n**Exact Macro Arithmetic**:\n- Formula: `Total kcal == (Protein * 4) + (Carbohydrate * 4) + (Fat * 9)`.\n- Recommended daily intake: 0.8g - 1.0g/kg body weight for sedentary adults; 1.2g - 1.6g/kg for fat loss; 1.6g - 2.0g/kg for muscle gain.\n\n*Clinical Note: In human digestion, consuming 20-35g protein per meal optimizes muscle protein synthesis.*"}if(A.e5(k,A.a(["egg","eggs","boiled egg","omelette","omelet","bhurji","egg white"],j)))return"### Exact Nutritional Breakdown: Poultry Eggs (Whole vs White)\n\n| Portion | Calories | Protein | Carbs | Fat | Key Micronutrients |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1 Whole Boiled Egg** (50g) | **72 kcal** | 6.3g | 0.4g | 5.0g | Choline, Lutein, B12, Vitamin D |\n| **2 Whole Boiled Eggs** (100g) | **144 kcal** | 12.6g | 0.8g | 10.0g | Complete amino acid profile |\n| **1 Egg White** (33g) | **17 kcal** | 3.6g | 0.2g | 0.1g | Pure bioavailable albumin |\n| **2-Egg Omelette** (with 1 tsp oil) | **185 kcal** | 13.0g | 1.5g | 14.0g | Vitamin A, Riboflavin, Selenium |\n\n**Clinical Mathematical Verification**:\n- 2 Whole Eggs: `(12.6 * 4) + (0.8 * 4) + (10.0 * 9) = 50.4 + 3.2 + 90 = 143.6 \u2248 144 kcal`.\n- Biological Value (BV): 100 (reference protein against which other proteins are judged).\n- Dietary Cholesterol: Contains ~186mg cholesterol per yolk. Clinical research confirms egg yolk cholesterol does not elevate cardiovascular risk in healthy individuals.\n\n*Clinical Note: Patients with familial hypercholesterolemia should limit whole yolks to 3-4 per week while consuming unrestricted egg whites.*"
if(A.e5(k,A.a(["chicken","fish","meat","mutton","prawn","chicken breast"],j)))return"### Exact Nutritional Science: Poultry & Lean Meats (ICMR & USDA)\n\n| Meat Item | Standard Portion | Calories | Protein | Carbs | Fat | Key Nutrients |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Chicken Breast** (Skinless, Cooked) | 100g Boneless | **165 kcal** | **31.0g** | 0.0g | 3.6g | Niacin, B6, Phosphorus |\n| **Chicken Thigh** (Skinless, Cooked) | 100g Boneless | **209 kcal** | **26.0g** | 0.0g | 10.9g | Zinc, Iron, Vitamin B12 |\n| **Rohu / Katla Fresh Fish** (Curry cut) | 100g Cooked | **128 kcal** | **19.5g** | 0.0g | 5.0g | Omega-3 EPA/DHA, Iodine |\n| **Mutton / Lean Goat Meat** | 100g Cooked | **230 kcal** | **25.0g** | 0.0g | 14.0g | Heme Iron, Bioavailable Zinc |\n\n**Clinical Preparation Rules**:\n- Prepare chicken and fish with homestyle curry (1 tsp mustard or groundnut oil, turmeric, garlic, ginger, tomatoes).\n- Avoid deep frying (adds 120-180 kcal of oxidized trans-fats per piece).\n- Macro Math for Chicken Breast: `(31 * 4) + (0 * 4) + (3.6 * 9) = 124 + 32.4 = 156.4 \u2248 165 kcal`.\n\n*Clinical Note: Fish consumed 2 times weekly significantly lowers systemic inflammation and triglyceride levels.*"
if(A.e5(k,A.a(["paneer","tofu","soya","soya chunks","soybean","soyabean"],j)))return"### Vegetarian Protein Sources: Paneer, Tofu & Soya (ICMR Validated)\n\n| Item | Standard Portion | Calories | Protein | Carbs | Fat | Calcium |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Fresh Cow Milk Paneer** | 100g Raw | **265 kcal** | **18.3g** | 3.5g | 20.8g | 480 mg |\n| **Low-Fat Paneer** | 100g Raw | **175 kcal** | **25.0g** | 4.0g | 6.0g | 520 mg |\n| **Soya Chunks** (Mealmaker) | 50g Dry (150g hydrated) | **172 kcal** | **26.0g** | 16.5g | 0.3g | 175 mg |\n| **Firm Tofu (Soy Curd)** | 100g Fresh | **83 kcal** | **10.0g** | 2.0g | 5.0g | 350 mg |\n\n**Clinical Insights**:\n- **Soya Chunks**: Has the highest protein density among plant staples (52g protein per 100g raw). Contains all essential amino acids (PDCAAS = 1.0).\n- **Paneer**: Rich in slowly digesting casein protein, making it an ideal pre-bed or dinner staple to prevent nighttime muscle breakdown.\n- **Tofu**: Low in saturated fat and calories, ideal for diabetes and cholesterol reduction.\n\n*Clinical Note: Soya does NOT cause feminization or hormonal imbalance in men when consumed in standard dietary portions (up to 50g dry chunks/day).*"
if(A.e5(k,A.a(["chapati","roti","phulka","rice","steamed rice","paratha","millets","jowar","ragi","oats"],j)))return"### Nutritional Comparison: Everyday Homely Grains (ICMR-IFCT Values)\n\n| Grain Item | Standard Portion | Calories | Protein | Carbs | Fat | Fiber |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Wheat Phulka** (No Oil) | 1 Medium (30g flour) | **75 kcal** | 3.0g | 15.0g | 0.5g | 2.3g |\n| **Wheat Chapati** (1/2 tsp oil) | 1 Medium (35g) | **95 kcal** | 3.0g | 15.0g | 2.5g | 2.3g |\n| **Steamed White Rice** | 1 Cup Cooked (150g) | **195 kcal** | 4.2g | 43.5g | 0.4g | 0.6g |\n| **Steamed Brown Rice** | 1 Cup Cooked (150g) | **180 kcal** | 4.5g | 38.0g | 1.5g | 3.2g |\n| **Jowar Roti (Sorghum)** | 1 Medium (40g) | **110 kcal** | 3.5g | 23.0g | 0.8g | 3.5g |\n| **Ragi Roti (Finger Millet)** | 1 Medium (40g) | **115 kcal** | 2.8g | 24.0g | 0.9g | 3.8g |\n| **Cooked Rolled Oats** | 1 Cup (40g raw) | **150 kcal** | 5.0g | 27.0g | 2.5g | 4.0g |\n\n**Clinical Guidance**:\n- Combining 1 cup dal with 2 phulkas or 1 cup rice creates a complementary amino acid profile (lysine + methionine) for a complete protein.\n- 2 Phulkas (150 kcal) offer 4x more dietary fiber than 1 cup polished white rice, significantly blunting the post-prandial glycemic spike.\n\n*Clinical Note: For optimal weight loss and diabetes control, replace 50% of white rice meals with unpolished millets or whole wheat phulkas.*"
if(A.e5(k,A.a(["dal","lentils","chana","chickpeas","rajma","moong","toor","urad","sprouts"],j)))return"### Clinical Nutritional Guide: Homely Indian Pulses & Legumes (ICMR-NIN)\n\n| Pulse Item | Standard Cooked Portion | Calories | Protein | Carbs | Fat | Fiber |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Toor / Arhar Dal** | 1 Cup (150g) | **148 kcal** | **8.5g** | 22.0g | 2.5g | 5.2g |\n| **Moong Dal (Yellow/Green)**| 1 Cup (150g) | **145 kcal** | **9.0g** | 21.5g | 2.0g | 5.5g |\n| **Boiled Kala Chana** | 1 Cup (150g) | **210 kcal** | **11.0g** | 35.0g | 3.0g | 8.0g |\n| **Boiled Rajma (Kidney Beans)**| 1 Cup (150g) | **215 kcal** | **12.0g** | 36.0g | 2.2g | 9.0g |\n| **Sprouted Moong Salad** | 1 Cup (100g raw sprouted)| **110 kcal** | **7.5g** | 18.0g | 0.8g | 4.5g |\n\n**Clinical Action Points**:\n- **Sprouting Advantage**: Sprouting green moong increases Vitamin C by 300% and bioavailability of zinc and iron by breaking down phytates.\n- **Gas & Flatulence Prevention**: Soak whole pulses (chana, rajma) for at least 8-10 hours; discard soak water and pressure cook with a pinch of hing (asafoetida) and ginger.\n\n*Clinical Note: Dal alone is not a pure protein source (it contains ~2.5x more carbs than protein). Pair dal with eggs, paneer, or curd for ideal macro balance.*"
if(A.e5(k,A.a(["curd","dahi","milk","buttermilk","majjiga","ghee","yogurt","dairy"],j)))return"### Nutritional Science: Everyday Homely Dairy (ICMR Standards)\n\n| Dairy Item | Standard Portion | Calories | Protein | Carbs | Fat | Calcium |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Fresh Homemade Curd (Dahi)**| 1 Cup (200g) | **122 kcal** | **6.5g** | 9.0g | 6.5g | 260 mg |\n| **Majjiga (Spiced Buttermilk)**| 1 Tall Glass (250ml) | **45 kcal** | **2.5g** | 3.5g | 2.2g | 180 mg |\n| **Cow Milk (Toned 3% fat)** | 1 Glass (200ml) | **116 kcal** | **6.2g** | 9.4g | 6.0g | 240 mg |\n| **Desi Cow Ghee** | 1 Teaspoon (5g) | **45 kcal** | 0.0g | 0.0g | **5.0g** | 0 mg |\n\n**Clinical Facts**:\n- **Curd vs Milk**: Curd undergoes lactic fermentation, breaking down lactose into lactic acid, making it easily digestible even for mildly lactose-intolerant individuals.\n- **Majjiga Therapy**: Diluted buttermilk with crushed curry leaves, ginger, and roasted jeera provides natural gut probiotics without caloric burden.\n- **Desi Ghee Truth**: Contains butyric acid that nourishes the colon lining. Safe in moderation (1-2 tsp daily), but calorie dense (45 kcal per single tsp).\n\n*Clinical Note: Avoid adding refined sugar to curd or buttermilk; consume with roasted cumin and pink salt instead.*"
if(A.e5(k,A.a(["weight loss","fat loss","lose belly","lose weight","slim","burn fat","calorie deficit","tdee","belly fat"],j)))return"### Clinical Evidence-Based Fat Loss & Metabolic Preservation Protocol\n\n| Nutritional Factor | Clinical Target | Mechanism & Clinical Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Caloric Deficit | 350 - 500 kcal below TDEE | Sustainable ~0.5kg/week fat reduction without muscle catabolism |\n| Protein Intake | 1.2g - 1.6g / kg ideal body weight | Maximizes satiety, high thermic effect of food (TEF: 20-30%) |\n| Fiber Target | 30g - 40g daily | Delays gastric emptying, stabilizes postprandial ghrelin |\n| Hydration | 3.0 - 3.5 Liters daily | Supports lipolysis and prevents dehydration-induced water retention |\n\n**Clinical Strategy**:\n- **Protein Distribution**: Never consume all protein in one meal. Aim for 20g - 30g protein in Breakfast, Lunch, and Dinner.\n- **Liquid Calories**: Eliminate sodas, packaged juices, and sugary coffees; liquid calories bypass brain satiety signaling.\n- **Meal Timing**: Finish dinner at least 2.5 to 3 hours before sleep to support nocturnal growth hormone and fat oxidation.\n\n*Clinical Note: Crash dieting (<1,200 kcal for men, <1,000 kcal for women) down-regulates thyroid conversion (T3) and causes rebound weight gain.*"
if(A.e5(k,A.a(["weight gain","muscle","bulk","gain weight","bodybuilding","hypertrophy","lean muscle"],j)))return"### Clinical Muscle Hypertrophy & Wholesome Weight Gain Protocol\n\n| Nutritional Factor | Clinical Target | Food Sources |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Caloric Surplus | +300 to +500 kcal above maintenance | Wholesome energy-dense foods, avoiding junk fats |\n| Protein Intake | 1.6g - 2.0g / kg body weight | Whole eggs, Paneer, Chicken breast, Soya, Kala chana |\n| Carbohydrates | 50-55% of total calories | Bananas, Steamed rice, Whole wheat chapatis, Sweet potato |\n| Healthy Fats | 25-30% of total calories | Peanut butter, Desi cow ghee, Almonds, Sesame seeds |\n\n**Clinical Strategy**:\n- **Nutrient-Dense Additions**: Add 1 spoon of peanut butter or 1 glass of whole milk with soaked almonds to your morning snack.\n- **Post-Workout Window**: Consume 20-25g fast-digesting protein (e.g. 3-4 egg whites or 1 bowl curd) within 45 minutes of training.\n- **Meal Frequency**: Eat 5 balanced meals per day spaced 3 hours apart to ensure a continuous amino acid pool.\n\n*Clinical Note: Gaining quality weight requires progressive resistance exercise alongside caloric surplus to stimulate muscle protein synthesis.*"
if(A.e5(k,A.a(["pcos","pcod","irregular periods","ovarian","cysts"],j)))return"### Clinical Nutrition Protocol for PCOS & Hormonal Balance\n\n| Nutritional Goal | Target | Recommended Foods |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Insulin Sensitization | Low Glycemic Load carbs | Steel-cut oats, Millets, Moong dal, Broccoli |\n| Anti-Inflammatory | Omega-3 & Polyphenols | Soaked walnuts, Flaxseed powder, Turmeric, Cinnamon |\n| Androgen Reduction | Natural Inositol & Zinc | Spearmint tea (2 cups/day), Pumpkin seeds, Sesame seeds |\n| Hormone Excretion | High Prebiotic Fiber (>35g) | Chia/Basil seeds, Methi leaves, Raw salads |\n\n**Clinical Action Points**:\n- **Spearmint Herbal Tea**: Studies demonstrate 2 cups of pure spearmint tea daily significantly lowers free testosterone in PCOS.\n- **Seed Cycling**: Phase 1 (Days 1-14): 1 tbsp pumpkin + 1 tbsp flax seeds. Phase 2 (Days 15-28): 1 tbsp sunflower + 1 tbsp sesame seeds.\n- **Dairy Quality**: Limit ultra-processed commercial dairy; prefer fresh homemade buttermilk or goat milk if sensitive.\n\n*Clinical Note: In PCOS, lifestyle and nutritional insulin regulation is 3x more effective for long-term cycle restoration than isolated supplements.*"
if(A.e5(k,A.a(["thyroid","hypothyroid","hypothyroidism","tsh","thyroxine","goitrogen"],j)))return"### Clinical Nutrition Protocol for Hypothyroidism & T3/T4 Conversion\n\n| Micronutrient | Purpose | Verified Food Sources |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Selenium | Essential for deiodinase (T4 to T3 conversion) | 2 Brazil nuts / week, Eggs, Sunflower seeds |\n| Zinc | Thyroid receptor binding & hormone synthesis | Pumpkin seeds, Lentils, Curd, Chicken |\n| Iodine | Structural backbone of thyroxine | Iodized table salt (adequate, not excessive) |\n| Tyrosine | Amino acid precursor of thyroid hormones | Sesame seeds, Paneer, Egg whites, Oats |\n\n**Clinical Precautions (Crucial)**:\n- **Goitrogen Cooking Rule**: Cruciferous vegetables (Cabbage, Cauliflower, Broccoli, Mustard greens) must **always be cooked thoroughly**; cooking deactivates goitrogenic glucosinolates.\n- **Medication Separation**: Take thyroxine on an empty stomach with plain water. **Wait at least 60 minutes** before having tea, coffee, breakfast, or calcium/iron supplements.\n\n*Clinical Note: Never consume raw soy or high-dose calcium within 4 hours of your morning thyroid medication.*"
if(A.e5(k,A.a(["cholesterol","triglycerides","lipid","ldl","hdl","heart diet","cardiac"],j)))return"### Clinical Cardiovascular & Lipid Optimization Protocol\n\n| Lipid Parameter | Clinical Goal | Dietary Action |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| LDL Cholesterol | < 100 mg/dL | Increase soluble fiber (Beta-glucan from oats, Psyllium husk 5g/day) |\n| Triglycerides | < 150 mg/dL | Strictly cut refined sugar, maida, and alcohol; consume Omega-3s |\n| HDL (Good) | > 40-50 mg/dL | Aerobic exercise, Virgin olive / mustard oil, Walnuts |\n| Trans Fats | 0.0g (Strict Zero) | Banish vanaspati, bakery puffs, reheated frying oils |\n\n**Clinical Action Points**:\n- **Soluble Fiber Mechanism**: 1 bowl of cooked oatmeal or 1 tsp isabgol binds bile acids in the intestine, forcing the liver to clear circulating LDL.\n- **Garlic Allium Therapy**: Consume 1-2 freshly crushed raw garlic cloves daily; allicin inhibits HMG-CoA reductase naturally.\n\n*Clinical Note: Triglycerides elevate predominantly from refined carbs and sugar, not dietary fat. Replacing refined carbs with healthy fats lowers triglycerides.*"
if(A.e5(k,A.a(["uric acid","gout","joint pain","purine","hyperuricemia"],j)))return"### Clinical Low-Purine Protocol for Hyperuricemia & Gout\n\n| Category | Strict Elimination | Allowed & Recommended |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Proteins | Red meat, Organ meats (liver/kidney), Shellfish, Sardines | Low-fat Curd/Milk, Egg whites, Moderate Moong dal |\n| Beverages | Beer, Whisky, Alcohol, High-Fructose Corn Syrup sodas | Water (3.5L/day), Fresh Lime water, Buttermilk |\n| Fruits | Dried fruits in excess, Sweetened canned fruit | Fresh Cherries, Guava, Indian Gooseberry (Amla) |\n\n**Clinical Action Points**:\n- **Alkaline Urine Flush**: Squeeze 1 fresh lemon into warm water twice daily; citric acid metabolizes into alkaline citrate, promoting renal uric acid clearance.\n- **Hydration Target**: Maintain at least 3.5 liters of clean water daily to prevent crystal crystallization in joint synovial fluid.\n\n*Clinical Note: Contrary to old myths, low-fat dairy actively accelerates renal uric acid excretion due to orotic acid and casein.*"
if(A.e5(k,A.a(["acidity","gerd","acid reflux","gastric","heartburn","bloating","indigestion"],j)))return"### Clinical Protocol for Acid Reflux (GERD) & Gastric Mucosa Healing\n\n| Trigger | Clinical Effect | Healing Alternative |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| High Spice / Red Chili | Irritates denuded gastric mucosa | Cumin (Jeera), Fennel (Saunf), Coriander |\n| Citrus on Empty Stomach | Stimulates excessive pepsin | Fresh coconut water, Soaked basil seeds |\n| Tea / Coffee on Empty Stomach | Relaxes lower esophageal sphincter (LES) | Warm water or ginger-infused herbal decoction |\n\n**Clinical Action Points**:\n- **Immediate Alkaline Relief**: 1 glass of cold buttermilk (Majjiga) with roasted cumin powder coats the stomach lining and neutralizes excess hydrochloric acid.\n- **Mechanical Rule**: Finish dinner at least 3 hours before lying down. Elevate head of bed by 15-20 degrees if nocturnal reflux occurs.\n- **Portion Control**: Never eat until 100% full; fill stomach 50% with food, 25% with water, and leave 25% empty for digestive churning.\n\n*Clinical Note: Chronic daily antacid use (PPIs) impairs B12 and calcium absorption. Treat the dietary root cause.*"
if(A.e5(k,A.a(["hair","hair fall","hair loss","anemia","hemoglobin","iron","biotin","ferritin","b12"],j)))return"### Clinical Protocol for Hair Follicle Nutrition, Iron & Hemoglobin Restoration\n\n| Micronutrient | Clinical Target | Top Homely Food Sources |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Elemental Iron & Ferritin | Boost serum ferritin > 50 ng/mL | Moringa (Drumstick) leaves, Roasted kala chana, Garden cress (Aliv) seeds, Dates |\n| Vitamin C (Iron Cofactor) | 80 - 100 mg with iron meals | Fresh Amla (1 daily), Lemon juice squeezed over dal/poha, Guava |\n| Biotin (Vitamin B7) & Zinc | Keratin synthesis | 2 Whole Boiled Eggs, Soaked almonds, Walnuts, Pumpkin seeds |\n| Complete Protein | 1.0 - 1.2g / kg body weight | Paneer, Moong dal, Eggs, Curd, Chicken / Fish |\n\n**Clinical Action Points**:\n- **Iron Absorption Rule**: Always pair plant iron (spinach, dal, chana) with fresh lemon juice. **Never drink tea or coffee within 60 minutes** of meals (tannins block 60% of iron absorption).\n- **Morning Follicle Tonic**: 1 tsp soaked Aliv (halim) seeds or 5 soaked almonds + 2 walnuts + 1 fresh amla every morning.\n- **Protein Sufficiency**: Hair shafts are 88% keratin protein; chronic low-protein diets trigger telogen effluvium (hair shedding)."
if(A.e5(k,A.a(["skin","acne","pimples","glow","collagen","pigmentation","anti aging"],j)))return"### Clinical Dermatological Nutrition Protocol (Skin Barrier & Collagen Synthesis)\n\n| Nutritional Factor | Mechanism | Verified Food Sources |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Low Glycemic Load | Prevents IGF-1 & sebum overproduction | Replace sugar, white bread & bakery snacks with whole grains & nuts |\n| Vitamin C + Proline | Pro-collagen hydroxylation | Amla, Guava, Orange, Bell peppers paired with Dal / Eggs |\n| Omega-3 & Vitamin E | Epidermal lipid barrier & UV defense | 1 tbsp Flaxseeds, Walnuts, Sunflower seeds, Avocado |\n| Zinc & Probiotics | Reduces *C. acnes* inflammation | Pumpkin seeds, Fresh homemade buttermilk (Majjiga) |\n\n**Clinical Action Points**:\n- **Acne Elimination**: Cut liquid dairy with added sugar (milkshakes, sweetened coffee, ice cream) for 21 days to lower sebaceous gland stimulation.\n- **Hydration & Carotenoids**: Consume 3L water daily plus 1 bowl of beta-carotene rich foods (carrots, papaya, tomatoes, spinach)."
if(A.e5(k,A.a(["pregnant","pregnancy","trimester","lactation","breastfeeding","postpartum","maternal"],j)))return"### Clinical Maternal & Fetal Nutrition Protocol (ICMR-NIN Standards)\n\n| Nutrient | Pregnancy Target | Key Homely Food Sources |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Energy & Protein | +350 kcal & +22g Protein (2nd/3rd Trimester) | Pasteurized Milk/Curd, Well-cooked Eggs, Dal, Paneer, Ragi |\n| Folate (Vitamin B9) | 500 mcg / day | Dark leafy greens (Palak, Methi), Rajma, Chana, Lentils |\n| Calcium & Vitamin D | 1,000 - 1,200 mg / day | Ragi malt, Curd, Paneer, Sesame (Til) seeds |\n| DHA & Choline | Fetal brain & retinal development | Walnuts, Pasteurized boiled eggs, Flaxseeds |\n\n**Clinical Safety Rules**:\n- **Strictly Avoid**: Unpasteurized milk, raw papaya, undercooked meat/eggs, excess caffeine (>150mg/day), and high-mercury fish.\n- **Nausea Management**: Small frequent meals every 2.5 hours; dry roasted chana or ginger-lemon infused water in the morning.\n\n*Clinical Note: Always take prescribed prenatal folic acid, iron, and calcium supplements as directed by your obstetrician.*"
if(A.e5(k,A.a(["fasting","intermittent fasting","16:8","autophagy","omad","fast"],j)))return"### Clinical Intermittent Fasting Protocol (16:8 Time-Restricted Feeding)\n\n| Window | Schedule | Allowed Intake |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Fasting Window (16h)** | 8:00 PM to 12:00 PM | Plain water, Warm jeera water, Unsweetened green tea / black coffee (0 kcal) |\n| **First Meal (12:00 PM)** | Gentle Insulin Break | 1 bowl salad + Protein (2 Eggs / Paneer / Moong Dal Chilla) + Complex carb |\n| **Snack (4:00 PM)** | Electrolyte & Satiety | Spiced Buttermilk (Majjiga) + Handful of roasted chana / almonds |\n| **Final Meal (7:30 PM)** | Early Circadian Dinner | 2 Phulkas / Khichdi + Dal/Chicken Curry + Steamed Vegetables |\n\n**Clinical Precautions**:\n- **Break Fast Gently**: Never break a 16-hour fast with refined sugar, fruit juice, or deep-fried snacks.\n- **Contraindications**: Avoid extended fasting if pregnant, breastfeeding, underweight (BMI < 18.5), or on sulfonylurea/insulin therapy without medical supervision."
if(A.e5(k,A.a(["workout","gym","pre workout","post workout","exercise","running","recovery","stamina"],j)))return"### Clinical Sports & Exercise Nutrition Protocol\n\n| Phase | Timing | Target Macros | Best Homely Options |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Pre-Workout** | 45 - 60 mins prior | 25-35g Carbs + Low Fat | 1 Banana + 5 Almonds, or 1 slice Toast with Peanut Butter, or Black Coffee |\n| **Intra-Workout** | During >60 min training | Electrolytes & Hydration | Plain water or Pinch of pink salt + lemon water |\n| **Post-Workout** | Within 45 mins after | 25-30g Protein + 30-40g Carbs | 3-4 Boiled Egg Whites + 1 Banana, or 100g Paneer/Chicken + Roti, or Soya/Whey |\n\n**Clinical Performance Rules**:\n- **Glycogen Replenishment**: Combining fast-absorbing protein with a moderate carbohydrate after resistance training increases muscle glycogen resynthesis by 40%.\n- **Cramp Prevention**: Ensure adequate potassium and magnesium from coconut water, bananas, and leafy greens."
if(A.e5(k,A.a(["sleep","insomnia","stress","cortisol","fatigue","tired","energy","weakness"],j)))return"### Clinical Sleep Architecture, Energy & Cortisol Regulation Protocol\n\n| Nutrient | Physiological Role | Evening Food Sources |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Tryptophan | Precursor to Serotonin & Melatonin | Warm milk with a pinch of nutmeg/turmeric, Curd, Banana, Oats |\n| Magnesium Glycinate | GABA receptor activation & muscle relaxation | Pumpkin seeds, Almonds, Spinach, Chamomile tea |\n| B-Complex & Iron | Cellular ATP energy synthesis | Whole grains, Eggs, Lentils, Roasted chana |\n\n**Clinical Action Points**:\n- **Caffeine Cutoff**: Stop all coffee, tea, and energy drinks at least **8 hours before bedtime** (caffeine half-life is 5-6 hours).\n- **Dinner-to-Bed Gap**: Finish a light, easily digestible dinner (like Moong Dal Khichdi or Phulkas with Sabzi) 2.5 to 3 hours before sleep."
if(A.e5(k,A.a(["constipation","gut","ibs","fiber","motions","digestion","piles","fissure"],j)))return"### Clinical Gut Motility & Microbiome Restoration Protocol\n\n| Component | Daily Target | Clinical Sources |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Soluble Fiber | Softens stool & feeds butyrate bacteria | 1-2 tsp Isabgol (Psyllium husk) in warm water, Oats, Papaya, Banana |\n| Insoluble Fiber | Increases colonic bulk & transit speed | Whole wheat bran, Green leafy vegetables, Guava with seeds, Salads |\n| Probiotics | Restores *Lactobacillus* & *Bifidobacterium* | Fresh homemade Curd, Spiced Buttermilk (Majjiga), Fermented Idli/Dosa |\n| Osmotic Hydration | 3.0 - 3.5 Liters water / day | 2 glasses warm water immediately upon waking |\n\n**Clinical Action Points**:\n- **Bedtime Relief**: Take 1 tsp Psyllium husk (Isabgol) or 5 soaked raisins/prunes with 1 glass of warm water at night.\n- **High Fiber Warning**: Increasing fiber without increasing water intake worsens constipation\u2014always drink 1 full glass of water with fiber-rich meals."
